*(
    title: Mathematics Extension 2
    description: The five areas of the course, what each one adds to Extension 1, and the order they are best taken in.
    tags: extension 2, course overview, proof, vectors, complex numbers, calculus, mechanics
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Extension 2 is taken alongside Extension 1 and assumes all of it. What it is not is Extension 1 with larger numbers in the questions.

    The difference is in what a question asks for. Extension 1 mostly hands over a statement and a method and asks for the method to be carried out. Extension 2 hands over a statement and asks whether it is true — a different job, which needs tools for establishing that something is false as much as tools for establishing that it is true.

    That is why Proof is an area of study here rather than a technique living inside one. It supplies the vocabulary the other four areas are written in.

    The table sets the five areas against what Extension 1 already gave you. Two of the rows are extensions of familiar work, two are the same subject carried into harder cases, and one is new outright.
}[
    table{
        Area            | In Extension 1                                | What Extension 2 adds
        Proof           | Induction, applied to sums and divisibility   | The language of proof itself, and the methods that show a statement false
        Vectors         | Vectors in two dimensions                     | A third dimension, lines in space, and geometric proof
        Complex numbers | Not in the course                             | The whole area
        Calculus        | Integration by substitution                   | Parts, partial fractions and reduction formulae
        Mechanics       | Projectile motion through a vacuum            | Oscillation, and motion against a resisting medium
    }(
        title: The five areas, measured against Extension 1
        header column
    )
]

scene{
    # The five areas of study

    Proof comes first because everything else leans on it. It replaces "show that" with a set of specific obligations: what an implication claims and what its converse claims instead, how a single counterexample disposes of a universal statement, and how assuming the opposite of what you want can establish it.

    internote(ref: the-nature-of-proof.chalk)

    ===

    Vectors and Complex numbers are the same manoeuvre carried out twice, and can be taken in either order. Vectors puts coordinates on three-dimensional space, so that questions about lines and angles become arithmetic. Complex numbers puts coordinates on the plane and then, unlike vectors, supplies a multiplication for them.

    internote(ref: vectors-in-three-dimensions.chalk)

    internote(ref: complex-numbers.chalk)

    ===

    Calculus is a toolkit rather than a theory: substitution generalised, and then parts, partial fractions and reduction formulae for the integrals substitution alone cannot reach.

    Mechanics is the only area with a hard prerequisite inside the course. It is calculus applied to a moving body, and it needs those integration techniques before it can do anything — which is why it belongs last, and why the diagram draws that one arrow solid while the arrows out of Proof are dashed.

    internote(ref: integration-by-substitution.chalk)

    internote(ref: velocity-as-a-function-of-displacement.chalk)
}[
    canvas.diagram{
        box#proof(label: Proof; sub: the language the other four are written in; shape: terminal)

        cue{
            box#vectors(label: Vectors; sub: coordinates on three-dimensional space)
            box#complex(label: Complex numbers; sub: coordinates on the plane, with a multiplication)
            box#calculus(label: Calculus; sub: techniques of integration)
            arrow(from: #proof; to: #vectors; dashed)
            arrow(from: #proof; to: #complex; dashed)
            arrow(from: #proof; to: #calculus; dashed)
        }(
            in: 1
        )

        cue.draw{
            box#mechanics(label: Mechanics; sub: calculus applied to a moving body)
            arrow(from: #calculus; to: #mechanics; label: needs the integration first)
        }(
            in: 2
        )
    }(
        flow: down
    )
]

scene{
    ## One point, two languages

    The canvas holds a single point, three units along and two up. Extension 2 gives it two names, and the difference between them is the reason both areas exist.

    As a complex number it is $3 + 2i$. As a vector it is $3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j}$. Nothing about the point distinguishes these; addition is identical in both, and so is the length.

    ===

    They part company over multiplication. There is no product of two plane vectors that is itself a plane vector — the dot product returns a number and throws the direction away. Two complex numbers do have such a product, and it turns out to be a rotation combined with a scaling. That single extra operation is what makes complex numbers the right language for plane geometry, and it is what the Complex numbers area spends its time on.

    Vectors buy something complex numbers cannot. The complex plane is two-dimensional and stays that way, while a vector takes a third component without complaint. So the two areas divide the work: rotation and multiplication in the plane go to complex numbers, and anything needing depth goes to vectors.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        point(x: 3; y: 2; colour: orange; label: $3 + 2i$)

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 3; dy: 2; colour: teal; label: $3\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j}$)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis, or $\mathbf{i}$
        y axis label: imaginary axis, or $\mathbf{j}$
    )
]
